WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:07.420
Ten years after Jumanji, we were introduced
to Zathura, an unrelated spinoff of the 1995

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.420
classic.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.450
Since it's a family adventure movie that both
kids and adults can watch together, the adults

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.630
are sometimes going to pick up on things that
the kids just don't see.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.590
These are the things only adults notice in
Zathura.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:27.320
Zathura begins by introducing us to the somewhat
compromised dynamic at work in the Budwing

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.090
family, as older brother Walter and younger
brother Danny bicker over who gets more time

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:35.620
with their dad, who gets control of the TV,
and more.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.370
In the process of introducing us to the family,
the film makes it clear that this environment

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.199
is the product of divorce.

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:46.840
This is made abundantly clear by the various
arguments Walter gets into with both his brother

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.120
and his father.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.320
At one point, when he learns that they're
about to be moved to his mother's house, Walter

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.469
gets angry, wishing he could stay at his father's
instead.

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:59.839
A few minutes later, though, when both Walter
and Danny point out that their father's house

00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:04.260
is "creepy," Walter seems to acknowledge that
his mother's house is just a better place

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.410
to hang out.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:09.130
And then, of course, there are the frequent
moments throughout the film in which an angry

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.970
Walter calls Danny the reason for all of their
problems, claiming that everything was fine

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.400
before he was born.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.580
Unless the kids watching have been through
it themselves, they might not really understand

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.810
the level of emotional confusion Walter's
going through.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.320
This is a kid who provokes conflict because,
deep down, he still doesn't fully understand

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.280
why his whole life had to change, and he wants
someone to blame.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.460
The opening minutes of Zathura are all about
setting things up for the story so that Walter

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.950
and Danny can be alone to play the game and
embark on their space adventure.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.210
With that in mind, their father is very quickly
hustled out the door to go to an important

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.800
meeting, and in lieu of calling another adult
to watch his sons, he instead wakes up his

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.650
teenage daughter, Lisa.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.670
Even if Lisa showed an interest in watching
her little brothers, which she doesn't, she

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:00.730
ends up cryogenically frozen for most of the
movie, and is therefore unable to help.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:05.240
And yet, adults in the room might notice a
detail that points to at least one other adult

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.060
who might have been in the picture.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.250
In the scene in which the Astronaut starts
eating all the food in the fridge, Danny points

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.250
out:

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.250
"So?

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.200
Lupe just went shopping."

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.459
Who is Lupe?

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:20.849
The implication is that she's a housekeeper,
but she could also be a relative or even the

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:22.430
father's romantic partner.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.540
So, if the father can afford a housekeeper,
why can't he also get a babysitter?

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.731
And if it's a romantic partner or relative
who's that involved in the household, why

00:02:31.731 --> 00:02:33.900
can't she be called at the moment?

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.459
Lupe could've stopped all of this before it
started.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.530
Like Jumanji, Zathura is the story of a magic
board game that physically affects the world

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.010
around its players.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.860
Unlike Jumanji, which brought elements of
a jungle setting to our world, Zathura literally

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.620
takes the players and the whole house they're
living in to space, creating a whole new set

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.790
of rules for how things might work in terms
of danger.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.120
On some level, because of the nature of the
film, it's easy to just not question this

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.120
new circumstance.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.040
The film even pokes fun at itself at one point
when Walter insists that the plumbing won't

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.499
work even as Danny fills a pot of water to
make macaroni and cheese.

00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:16.680
While kids are invested in the magic of it,
though, the adults in the room probably notice

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.780
that not all Zathura threats are created equal.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.549
For one thing, the house seems to maintain
its own gravity field despite being teleported

00:03:24.549 --> 00:03:25.879
into outer space.

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:30.249
For another, whole walls can be ripped off
of the structure without dragging the occupants

00:03:30.249 --> 00:03:35.329
out into the void, and yet when they pass
by a sun, the gravity field is enough to nearly

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:36.329
kill them.

00:03:36.329 --> 00:03:40.799
It's all in service to the story, of course,
but Walter and Danny are very lucky that their

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:44.700
house only responds to some aspects of danger
in space.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:51.050
Eventually, after surviving a number of other
threats, Walter and Danny meet the Astronaut,

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:55.650
a stranded spaceman who's drawn to the house
when the game gives them a card saying they're

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.340
supposed to rescue him.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.549
As we spend more time with the Astronaut,
we learn more about his backstory.

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:05.659
He was playing the game with his brother,
made a wish on a shooting star that his brother

00:04:05.659 --> 00:04:10.560
was never born, and was therefore stranded
without any way to get home because the other

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.840
player, his brother, could never take their
turn with the game.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.100
In the course of explaining his life since
then, the Astronaut mentions that he's been

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:23.110
eating "paste" from a tube for years, and
he's managed to gain quite a bit of know-how

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.980
about how to survive out in deep space.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.730
What he doesn't mention is how he managed
to survive those initial days, weeks, and

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.250
months, particularly since he was a little
boy at the time.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.470
Was he rescued by other space explorers?

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.470
Where are they?

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.750
How did he end up with a spacesuit?

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.970
Did the game provide those things and allow
him to improvise?

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:46.970
Whatever happened, he'd apparently be very
good in some kind of Man vs. Wild-in-space

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:47.970
situation.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.220
Walter and Danny face a number of threats
during their time playing Zathura, from a

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.020
meteor shower to a malfunctioning robot to
the gravity well of a sun.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.030
However, the most dangerous of these threats
turns out to be the Zorgons -- a race of alien

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:08.340
scavengers who show up and seem ready to rip
the house to shreds until the Astronaut drives

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.120
them away with the help of a burning couch.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.750
As the Astronaut explains, the Zorgons are
cold-blooded lizard creatures who scavenge

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.320
the galaxy for any kind of heat.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.590
They're drawn to things like lights and appliances
and heating elements, so to get rid of them,

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:28.160
Walter and Danny must turn off every light
and extinguish every flame in the house -- even

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.900
the tiniest pilot light.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.740
"Turn off all the lights and electrical appliances."

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:34.740
"Me?"

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.740
"Yes, you, go!

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:36.740
Do it!"

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.300
For kids, this makes perfect sense, but the
adults in the room might have questions.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:45.200
After all, the Astronaut insists that every
heat source has to be turned off, but he never

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.550
mentions body heat.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:51.490
If a pilot light on a furnace in the basement
can draw these things, why can't several living,

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.570
breathing humans clustered together in a high-stress
situation?

00:05:55.570 --> 00:06:00.080
When we first meet the Astronaut, he's portrayed
as a mysterious figure from space who just

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.660
happens to be summoned into the house because
Danny and Walter pull the right card.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:09.240
Eventually, though, the reason he knows so
much about the game and about them becomes

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.760
clear: He's actually a future version of Walter,
15 years older, who got stranded in space

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.060
after he wished on a star that Danny had never
been born.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.830
The Astronaut only reveals this to the boys
after Walter gets a second wish card, and

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.800
wishes that the Astronaut could have his brother
back.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.260
This produces a doppelganger of Danny, the
truth comes out, and both the Astronaut and

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:34.150
the other Danny fade away as the timelines
are reconciled.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.840
It's a sweet moment, but the adults in the
room might've noticed something interesting

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:38.990
about the event.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:44.000
By talking Walter out of using his first wish
to wish Danny away, the Astronaut actually

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.590
prevented his own sad future from happening
at all.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:51.950
There was no need for a second wish to bring
Danny back because Danny never left.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:56.751
Therefore, if this is a film that posits that
the alternate future is simply erased, it

00:06:56.751 --> 00:07:01.760
should've probably been erased the moment
young Walter decided he wanted Danny to stay.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:05.080
Time travel just tends to make things a little
loopy that way.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:10.530
Zathura is the story of two brothers who are
caught up in an adventure together.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:14.420
Walter and Danny begin the movie at odds with
one another, get caught up in playing the

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:18.310
game, and have to learn to work together to
get back home safely.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:22.781
The ultimate message of the movie — that
family is about love and cooperation, even

00:07:22.781 --> 00:07:27.460
amid adversity — is still quite clear, but
the adults watching the film may have noticed

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:28.710
something else.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:33.090
Walter and Danny are playing the game, but
their older sister, Lisa, is also stranded

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:38.750
in space with them, and she eventually even
wakes up from cryogenic stasis to help them

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:39.750
survive.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.620
The Astronaut ultimately reveals himself to
be future Walter, who got stranded when Danny

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.900
was wished away, but if that's true then what
happened to Lisa in the Astronaut's timeline?

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:53.090
It's possible that the game didn't let her
take Danny's place after he vanished, but

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.120
even if that's true, she should've also been
alive in the house, right?

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.120
Did she die?

00:07:58.120 --> 00:07:59.460
Did she get taken by aliens?

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:03.830
For adults, these are questions that just
can't be glossed over for the sake of fun

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:04.830
and easy entertainment.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:10.720
Zathura is a successful film experience in
part because it delivers exactly what its

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.590
trailers promised — a fun, family-friendly
space adventure.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:19.930
What really puts the film over the top, though,
is the way it works as a story about family,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.230
destiny, and love.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:25.930
When Walter and Danny draw the astronaut card
while playing the game, they expect just another

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:31.150
random sci-fi encounter, and when the Astronaut
shows up, he at first presents in exactly

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.150
that way.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:36.569
As the film goes on, though, we find out that
he's actually an adult Walter from the future,

00:08:36.569 --> 00:08:41.529
and he's come back to convince his younger
self not to destroy his family out of spite.

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:46.370
This is particularly interesting because every
other encounter in the game is still random,

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:50.029
which implies there was a reason for that
card at that moment.

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:55.020
It seems that Zathura itself, whether out
of a desire to finish the game or some self-aware

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:59.829
sense of love, sent Walter back to fix his
mistake and repair his family.

00:08:59.829 --> 00:09:04.430
For the adults in the room, this is a subtle
and comforting message -- that sometimes the

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:07.850
universe takes over and sorts things out on
its own.

